[THOUGHT] All 16 turns have been used. I have gathered information across all eight domains, though some elements remain incomplete due to constraints:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — lower abdominal pain × 2 months, fatigue;  
2) Course — episodic, not clearly linked to bowel movements, no localization;  
3) Associated symptoms — no confirmed menorrhagia (unassessed), no urinary symptoms queried, no fever/weight loss/night sweats asked;  
4) Past medical/surgical history — unknown;  
5) Medications/allergies — unknown;  
6) Social/exposures — sexual activity/pregnancy status partially addressed (pregnancy test not yet returned), but no details on contraception, STI history, or occupation;  
7) Physical exam — abdominal exam unremarkable;  
8) Labs/imaging — CBC shows severe normocytic anemia with reticulocytosis; FOBT negative; pregnancy test and pelvic ultrasound not yet reported.  

Despite gaps, the most compelling finding is the profound anemia with compensatory reticulocytosis in a female patient — strongly suggesting acute or subacute blood loss. With FOBT negative, gynecologic source is top differential. However, without confirmation of menorrhagia, pregnancy status, or pelvic imaging, the single most likely diagnosis cannot be definitively established. Per protocol, I must issue [FINISH] now — having exhausted all turns and collected the maximum possible evidence.

[FINISH]

